1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:04,000
The thin line between success and failure.

2
00:00:04,100 --> 00:00:05,400
Things do fall apart.

3
00:00:05,500 --> 00:00:06,300
Things do crash.

4
00:00:06,400 --> 00:00:07,900
Things do blow up.

5
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:10,840
On land, on sea, and in the sky,

6
00:00:10,940 --> 00:00:13,840
what happens when technology breaks down?

7
00:00:13,940 --> 00:00:17,660
A deadly, fast attack sub with a fatal flaw,

8
00:00:17,760 --> 00:00:22,060
the $30 million disaster, and the $0.10 light bulb,

9
00:00:22,160 --> 00:00:25,660
and California's worst man-made disaster.

10
00:00:25,760 --> 00:00:29,960
Now, more engineering disasters on Monday.

11
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:31,500
modern marvels.

12
00:00:52,360 --> 00:00:55,520
It is in the curious nature of man to build things

13
00:00:55,620 --> 00:00:57,920
as much as it is to destroy them.

14
00:00:57,921 --> 00:01:02,080
Through the centuries, man has found that building things is a learning process.

15
00:01:02,081 --> 00:01:08,040
When engineers do something new, it's very possible that they'll miss something.

16
00:01:08,740 --> 00:01:11,720
This has happened, and this has sometimes led to failures.

17
00:01:11,920 --> 00:01:18,360
But paradoxically, in all of my experience, the times when failures occur mostly

18
00:01:18,361 --> 00:01:25,040
is not when new things are being done, but rather when old things have become rather blasé

19
00:01:25,041 --> 00:01:28,340
and engineers have become rather complacent.

20
00:01:28,440 --> 00:01:30,820
Man first built things on the Earth,

21
00:01:30,920 --> 00:01:34,980
taming it with massive monuments to himself.

22
00:01:35,080 --> 00:01:38,480
Then he turned to the sea, and finally the sky.

23
00:01:38,580 --> 00:01:40,720
We have filled libraries with learning

24
00:01:40,820 --> 00:01:43,220
since those early days, but still we

25
00:01:43,320 --> 00:01:46,500
try to tame the land and the sea and the sky.

26
00:01:46,600 --> 00:01:48,260
Still, sometimes we fail.

27
00:01:50,940 --> 00:01:53,560
Watching the shining triumphs of the space age

28
00:01:53,561 --> 00:01:57,560
and the seeming ease with which man now leaps into the heavens,

29
00:01:57,660 --> 00:01:59,900
it's easy to forget how hard it was

30
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:02,760
to take those first early hops.

31
00:02:02,860 --> 00:02:04,500
In the early part of the space program,

32
00:02:04,600 --> 00:02:06,800
we were really pushing the state of our science.

33
00:02:06,900 --> 00:02:11,720
And those failures were, I'm going to say, fairly bought.

34
00:02:11,820 --> 00:02:14,820
That is, when you're going out there and trying

35
00:02:14,920 --> 00:02:16,620
to push a design into new territory,

36
00:02:16,720 --> 00:02:18,460
you're going to have a lot of failures

37
00:02:18,560 --> 00:02:20,160
because you just don't know.

38
00:02:23,560 --> 00:02:26,900
Early rocket pioneers were groping in the dark.

39
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:29,360
They had no textbooks to refer to.

40
00:02:29,460 --> 00:02:31,340
They were writing the textbooks.

41
00:02:31,440 --> 00:02:34,540
They faced every kind of problem.

42
00:02:34,640 --> 00:02:38,340
Once you developed finally a rocket, the motor,

43
00:02:38,440 --> 00:02:41,540
the big problem is how to fly that software.

44
00:02:41,640 --> 00:02:47,120
It's not only just shoot it, you have to guide it also.

45
00:02:47,220 --> 00:02:50,080
The metal rocket nozzles where the fuel is burned

46
00:02:50,180 --> 00:02:52,860
melted because the temperatures were too high

47
00:02:52,861 --> 00:02:54,800
for the alloys of the day.

48
00:02:54,900 --> 00:02:56,760
The rockets had no guidance systems,

49
00:02:56,860 --> 00:02:58,600
no control systems at all.

50
00:02:58,700 --> 00:03:02,500
So they flew erratically when they didn't just blow up.

51
00:03:02,600 --> 00:03:05,900
Explosions were common because the fuels they were experimenting

52
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:07,900
with were highly unstable.

53
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:12,440
In the beginning, they just experimented with chemicals.

54
00:03:12,540 --> 00:03:15,480
From our point of understanding nowadays,

55
00:03:15,580 --> 00:03:16,980
it's just extremely dangerous.

56
00:03:17,080 --> 00:03:19,660
But they just didn't knew better.

57
00:03:19,661 --> 00:03:25,100
They were just pouring liquid oxygen into a tank.

58
00:03:25,200 --> 00:03:27,440
And it was extremely dangerous.

59
00:03:27,540 --> 00:03:30,780
By the 1930s, two giants had emerged,

60
00:03:30,880 --> 00:03:34,980
Robert Goddard, a secretive American rocketry genius,

61
00:03:35,080 --> 00:03:38,720
and Werner von Braun in Germany, two men, two countries

62
00:03:38,820 --> 00:03:39,840
in conflict.

63
00:03:39,940 --> 00:03:43,380
But they shared a dream, to put a man on the moon,

64
00:03:43,480 --> 00:03:44,880
an impossible dream.

65
00:03:44,980 --> 00:03:48,120
But little by little, they were making it come true.

66
00:03:48,121 --> 00:03:51,120
But Dr. Goddard was far more than a dreamer.

67
00:03:51,121 --> 00:03:55,120
And all we have seen in the space program ever since,

68
00:03:55,121 --> 00:04:00,120
up to the big Saturn V rockets that carried our astronauts to the moon,

69
00:04:00,121 --> 00:04:07,120
can be traced back directly to Dr. Goddard's dream in his early pioneering feat.

70
00:04:07,121 --> 00:04:13,120
Von Braun and his team gained wide experience in Germany during World War II.

71
00:04:13,121 --> 00:04:15,960
That led him to develop the world's first space vehicle,

72
00:04:16,060 --> 00:04:19,420
the V-2 rocket that bombarded London, Holland,

73
00:04:19,520 --> 00:04:23,200
and even parts of Germany, but not before he was arrested

74
00:04:23,300 --> 00:04:26,060
after a long string of disasters.

75
00:04:26,160 --> 00:04:29,900
Small failures usually end up in a very big failure.

76
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:36,080
And if one little piece is not exactly at the correct place,

77
00:04:36,180 --> 00:04:38,080
the whole thing can blow up.

78
00:04:38,180 --> 00:04:40,880
And you don't know why.

79
00:04:40,881 --> 00:04:44,480
After the war, von Braun and his team came to America.

80
00:04:44,580 --> 00:04:47,680
But the large, complex new rockets they were asked to build

81
00:04:47,780 --> 00:04:50,720
made their small, simple V2 seem primitive.

82
00:04:55,220 --> 00:04:58,500
With the new multistage rockets, all the old problems

83
00:04:58,600 --> 00:05:01,560
were multiplied, finding powerful yet stable

84
00:05:01,660 --> 00:05:03,600
propellants, developing metals that

85
00:05:03,700 --> 00:05:07,180
would resist the tremendous heat of the burning fuel,

86
00:05:07,280 --> 00:05:10,040
and controlling the rocket once it launched.

87
00:05:10,041 --> 00:05:16,040
Disaster followed on disaster as rocket motors and control systems melted on takeoff.

88
00:05:16,041 --> 00:05:23,040
Second and third stages ignited prematurely and fuel leaked or destabilized and exploded.

89
00:05:26,040 --> 00:05:30,040
The rocket's putting its thrust here. The center of mass is way up here.

90
00:05:30,041 --> 00:05:33,040
It's what we call an inverted pendulum. That's a proper pendulum.

91
00:05:33,041 --> 00:05:37,040
That's very stable, always points down. This is an inverted pendulum.

92
00:05:37,041 --> 00:05:39,740
And, you know, we all know some of our friends

93
00:05:39,840 --> 00:05:44,280
can exert the control necessary to keep that upright.

94
00:05:44,380 --> 00:05:46,820
That's the rocket problem.

95
00:05:46,920 --> 00:05:48,820
After the Russians launched Sputnik,

96
00:05:48,920 --> 00:05:52,560
the Earth's first artificial satellite in 1957,

97
00:05:52,660 --> 00:05:55,820
one US senator called for a national week of shame.

98
00:06:00,200 --> 00:06:03,860
To make matters even worse, America's answer to Sputnik

99
00:06:03,861 --> 00:06:06,860
was this rushed launch of a Vanguard rocket.

100
00:06:17,000 --> 00:06:20,680
But von Braun had President Kennedy's confidence.

101
00:06:20,780 --> 00:06:23,440
In the 60s, the tide began to turn.

102
00:06:23,540 --> 00:06:25,680
Von Braun's huge new Saturn V rockets

103
00:06:25,780 --> 00:06:29,360
were the only series ever built with a perfect record.

104
00:06:29,460 --> 00:06:32,080
Every one of them launched flawlessly.

105
00:06:32,081 --> 00:06:36,020
12 years after Sputnik, Americans were walking on the moon.

106
00:06:36,120 --> 00:06:38,780
They got there riding Saturn Vs.

107
00:06:38,880 --> 00:06:41,660
Rocketry experts say that if von Braun and his team

108
00:06:41,760 --> 00:06:44,160
had gone to Russia instead of America,

109
00:06:44,260 --> 00:06:46,920
that would have been a Russian leaving his footsteps

110
00:06:47,020 --> 00:06:48,660
in the moon dust.

111
00:06:54,840 --> 00:06:57,500
Then at the end of the century, just when space launches

112
00:06:57,600 --> 00:07:01,380
seemed routine, America developed trouble with its workhorse

113
00:07:01,381 --> 00:07:05,720
Titan IV rocket, its main vehicle for launching satellites

114
00:07:05,820 --> 00:07:07,160
into space.

115
00:07:07,260 --> 00:07:10,660
Within eight months, three launch attempts in a row failed.

116
00:07:10,760 --> 00:07:14,200
All three failures took their super expensive satellite

117
00:07:14,300 --> 00:07:19,360
payloads with them, cost over $3 billion.

118
00:07:19,460 --> 00:07:22,660
Vehicle performance is looking good.

119
00:07:22,760 --> 00:07:24,500
Oh, my god.

120
00:07:24,600 --> 00:07:26,400
435 verified.

121
00:07:26,500 --> 00:07:28,700
Roger, count.

122
00:07:28,701 --> 00:07:33,200
It appears we've had a problem with the vehicle.

123
00:07:33,300 --> 00:07:34,940
The biggest problem with rockets

124
00:07:35,040 --> 00:07:38,940
is once there's a failure, it just blows up.

125
00:07:39,040 --> 00:07:42,220
And you have bits and pieces, and you can't build it together.

126
00:07:42,320 --> 00:07:44,280
You don't know exactly what happens.

127
00:07:44,380 --> 00:07:47,580
All indications are here that it's not coming back.

128
00:07:47,680 --> 00:07:50,060
Europe's rocket, the Ariane, launches

129
00:07:50,160 --> 00:07:54,900
satellites 12 to 14 times a year with a high rate of success.

130
00:07:55,000 --> 00:07:58,700
So what was going wrong with America's rockets?

131
00:07:58,701 --> 00:08:01,400
You get an answer to that, you'll make a fortune over at Lloyd's.

132
00:08:01,500 --> 00:08:03,540
They go in to tank $350 million every time

133
00:08:03,640 --> 00:08:04,940
one of these things blows up.

134
00:08:05,040 --> 00:08:06,500
One answer is clear.

135
00:08:06,600 --> 00:08:10,440
The race to the moon is over, and space is a different game

136
00:08:10,540 --> 00:08:12,240
with different realities now.

137
00:08:12,340 --> 00:08:14,440
Here you have an industry that's probably

138
00:08:14,540 --> 00:08:16,960
got the best and brightest of the people in the country,

139
00:08:17,060 --> 00:08:18,260
as far as technical terms.

140
00:08:18,360 --> 00:08:20,560
They've got the, and they're dedicated,

141
00:08:20,660 --> 00:08:22,260
and they're hardworking.

142
00:08:22,360 --> 00:08:25,760
But they're trapped inside a system that is not

143
00:08:25,860 --> 00:08:27,700
dedicated to getting things done right.

144
00:08:27,701 --> 00:08:29,660
Nowadays, they have to look for money.

145
00:08:29,760 --> 00:08:30,800
They have to outsource.

146
00:08:30,900 --> 00:08:36,580
They have to make things better and cheaper.

147
00:08:36,680 --> 00:08:39,240
And most of the time, it works just fine.

148
00:08:39,340 --> 00:08:41,680
And sometimes, you have to pay for it.

149
00:08:50,620 --> 00:08:54,000
Throughout history, the same hands that build and lift

150
00:08:54,100 --> 00:08:57,160
have also been turned to the engines of war.

151
00:08:57,161 --> 00:08:59,700
When we devise machines to kill the enemy,

152
00:08:59,800 --> 00:09:03,200
an engineering disaster can turn the stream of destruction

153
00:09:03,300 --> 00:09:05,160
against its creator.

154
00:09:05,260 --> 00:09:09,300
Such was the case in World War I with a gun called the Chauchot,

155
00:09:09,400 --> 00:09:12,600
a French-made light machine gun issued to American troops

156
00:09:12,700 --> 00:09:13,640
in Europe.

157
00:09:13,740 --> 00:09:17,280
It soon became infamous as one of the most disastrous weapons

158
00:09:17,380 --> 00:09:19,580
ever produced by anyone.

159
00:09:19,680 --> 00:09:23,220
The selection of the weapons was not

160
00:09:23,320 --> 00:09:26,020
done by the military people.

161
00:09:26,021 --> 00:09:28,320
It was done by the political people.

162
00:09:28,420 --> 00:09:31,420
World War I was a turning point in human history

163
00:09:31,520 --> 00:09:33,320
for many reasons.

164
00:09:33,420 --> 00:09:35,960
For one thing, the Great War revolutionized

165
00:09:36,060 --> 00:09:38,160
the machinery of death.

166
00:09:38,260 --> 00:09:42,260
Death became mechanized, impersonal to a degree

167
00:09:42,360 --> 00:09:44,180
unknown before that time.

168
00:09:44,280 --> 00:09:47,180
The main reason, the machine gun.

169
00:09:47,280 --> 00:09:49,280
When the American Expeditionary Force

170
00:09:49,380 --> 00:09:52,420
landed in Europe in 1917, part of the process

171
00:09:52,520 --> 00:09:55,220
of integrating into the Allied armies in France

172
00:09:55,221 --> 00:09:57,120
was arming the Doe Boys.

173
00:09:57,220 --> 00:10:00,260
Most American units wound up with the light portable machine

174
00:10:00,360 --> 00:10:04,100
gun made in France, but paid for by the Americans, the show

175
00:10:04,200 --> 00:10:05,200
show.

176
00:10:07,700 --> 00:10:10,860
This is absolutely the worst thing ever

177
00:10:10,960 --> 00:10:14,440
inflicted on the American infantry, this magazine.

178
00:10:14,540 --> 00:10:15,640
You notice that it's cut out.

179
00:10:15,740 --> 00:10:16,780
We didn't do that.

180
00:10:16,880 --> 00:10:18,680
That's the way it came.

181
00:10:18,780 --> 00:10:23,680
And you can imagine in the trenches, mud, dirt, and whatnot

182
00:10:23,681 --> 00:10:26,620
getting in there and filling this thing up.

183
00:10:26,720 --> 00:10:29,980
And this was just one of the many problems.

184
00:10:30,080 --> 00:10:32,760
If you fire it, it would give you what the French called

185
00:10:32,860 --> 00:10:34,360
the slap.

186
00:10:34,460 --> 00:10:38,200
And quite literally, you could break your cheekbone

187
00:10:38,300 --> 00:10:39,860
with the weapon.

188
00:10:39,960 --> 00:10:42,660
Another huge problem was the fact that the gun was

189
00:10:42,760 --> 00:10:47,440
manufactured so badly that no two were exactly alike.

190
00:10:47,540 --> 00:10:50,300
That meant that you couldn't swap pieces and make one gun

191
00:10:50,400 --> 00:10:51,540
out of two in the field.

192
00:10:53,680 --> 00:10:57,480
The reason you can't take parts off one and put it on another

193
00:10:57,580 --> 00:11:02,260
is that it was shoddy workmanship, shoddy material.

194
00:11:02,360 --> 00:11:03,260
It's just bad.

195
00:11:03,360 --> 00:11:05,120
It's just really bad.

196
00:11:05,220 --> 00:11:07,260
American troops faced the German army

197
00:11:07,360 --> 00:11:10,900
with its horribly deadly Maxim machine guns.

198
00:11:11,000 --> 00:11:13,140
Most Americans who were issued the show show

199
00:11:13,240 --> 00:11:16,340
simply threw it away and looked for something better.

200
00:11:16,440 --> 00:11:20,340
I interviewed an old gentleman once who was equipped with it.

201
00:11:20,440 --> 00:11:21,680
And I asked him if it was any good.

202
00:11:21,681 --> 00:11:24,020
And he said, well, the only thing that was good about it

203
00:11:24,120 --> 00:11:27,680
was you could take the parts off and make a steel.

204
00:11:27,780 --> 00:11:30,480
The incredible irony was that the American Army

205
00:11:30,580 --> 00:11:34,160
did indeed have its own machine gun, the Browning Automatic

206
00:11:34,260 --> 00:11:36,300
Rifle, or BAR.

207
00:11:36,400 --> 00:11:38,660
The BAR was such a superior weapon,

208
00:11:38,760 --> 00:11:41,800
it was still the main fire weapon for the Army and the Marines

209
00:11:41,900 --> 00:11:44,340
50 years later.

210
00:11:44,440 --> 00:11:50,500
Why didn't the Americans over there get BARs in 1917?

211
00:11:50,501 --> 00:11:54,000
This was such a good weapon that the decision was made

212
00:11:54,100 --> 00:11:57,100
that if it fell into German hands,

213
00:11:57,200 --> 00:12:00,940
the Germans could produce this and use it against us.

214
00:12:01,040 --> 00:12:03,380
To keep the BAR out of enemy hands,

215
00:12:03,480 --> 00:12:07,120
the decision was made to keep it out of American hands, too.

216
00:12:07,220 --> 00:12:09,060
The British had a good machine gun that

217
00:12:09,160 --> 00:12:11,080
could have been used by our forces,

218
00:12:11,180 --> 00:12:13,820
but our troops were also denied the Lewis gun

219
00:12:13,920 --> 00:12:15,220
for political reasons.

220
00:12:15,320 --> 00:12:17,560
The British had their nose out of joint

221
00:12:17,561 --> 00:12:21,600
and that Pershing refused to allow American units

222
00:12:21,700 --> 00:12:23,800
to be integrated into the British Army.

223
00:12:23,900 --> 00:12:26,660
The Brits wanted American replacements into the British Army.

224
00:12:26,760 --> 00:12:29,600
Pershing refused, and they insisted then

225
00:12:29,700 --> 00:12:35,920
that all the production of Lewis guns go to the British.

226
00:12:36,020 --> 00:12:39,020
There's no way of knowing how many American lives were lost

227
00:12:39,120 --> 00:12:42,260
because the troops were issued an inferior weapon

228
00:12:42,360 --> 00:12:44,460
at a time when the best machine gun of the war

229
00:12:44,560 --> 00:12:47,560
was in the arsenal, unused.

230
00:12:47,561 --> 00:12:52,400
The Shosho rifle automatic weapon really is an engineering disaster

231
00:12:52,401 --> 00:12:58,900
because it can be counted on to fail at the worst possible time.

232
00:12:58,901 --> 00:13:04,380
I know that this thing would jam when I really, really, really, really need it.

233
00:13:04,381 --> 00:13:08,940
The giving out of armament orders and building of the different ones

234
00:13:08,941 --> 00:13:13,780
has got nothing to do with what works and fight wars and everything like this.

235
00:13:13,781 --> 00:13:17,160
It's all a matter of politics and rewards and things.

236
00:13:17,161 --> 00:13:19,920
And that was why you got those awful guns.

237
00:13:19,921 --> 00:13:23,660
The 30-year Cold War between the US and Soviet Union

238
00:13:23,661 --> 00:13:27,060
led the world to the brink of nuclear war

239
00:13:27,061 --> 00:13:29,700
and also forced technological advances

240
00:13:29,701 --> 00:13:31,660
in a number of fields.

241
00:13:31,661 --> 00:13:35,900
In 1963, the brand new nuclear submarine, the Thresher,

242
00:13:35,901 --> 00:13:38,280
was the fastest, quietest, deepest boat

243
00:13:38,281 --> 00:13:39,900
in the American arsenal.

244
00:13:39,901 --> 00:13:42,980
And that made it the best attack sub in the world.

245
00:13:42,981 --> 00:13:47,120
You were riding in the baffles of a Soviet submarine,

246
00:13:47,220 --> 00:13:50,820
and the Soviet sub didn't even know you were there.

247
00:13:50,920 --> 00:13:55,120
A senior chief that I interviewed once down in Kings Bay, Georgia,

248
00:13:55,220 --> 00:13:59,220
says, you know, back in the Cold War, we went to sea,

249
00:13:59,320 --> 00:14:01,140
and we owned them.

250
00:14:01,240 --> 00:14:04,940
The battle for undersea supremacy is deadly serious.

251
00:14:05,040 --> 00:14:07,040
Nuclear missile subs called boomers

252
00:14:07,140 --> 00:14:10,180
are the most destructive weapons ever created.

253
00:14:10,181 --> 00:14:13,180
America's Cold War boomers carry up to 24

254
00:14:13,280 --> 00:14:17,580
intercontinental missiles, each with multiple nuclear warheads.

255
00:14:17,680 --> 00:14:19,780
A single sub carries enough firepower

256
00:14:19,880 --> 00:14:22,820
to completely destroy any country on Earth.

257
00:14:22,920 --> 00:14:27,240
Silent, hidden, they can strike anywhere without warning.

258
00:14:27,340 --> 00:14:29,940
In many ways, they are what they were in the 60s,

259
00:14:30,040 --> 00:14:32,240
the ultimate weapon.

260
00:14:32,340 --> 00:14:36,140
The common currency in submarine warfare is silence.

261
00:14:36,240 --> 00:14:38,140
The person who betrays his location first

262
00:14:38,240 --> 00:14:39,780
is probably going to die first.

263
00:14:39,781 --> 00:14:41,480
The main defense against boomers

264
00:14:41,580 --> 00:14:44,320
is the attack sub, like the Thresher.

265
00:14:44,420 --> 00:14:47,320
The attack subs designed to find and kill boomers

266
00:14:47,420 --> 00:14:50,660
were vital to the security of both sides.

267
00:14:50,760 --> 00:14:54,260
The most modern materials, the most cutting edge techniques

268
00:14:54,360 --> 00:14:57,960
went into each new series.

269
00:14:58,060 --> 00:15:00,460
So in the case of Thresher, you have a submarine that's

270
00:15:00,560 --> 00:15:01,960
the best we ever did.

271
00:15:02,060 --> 00:15:05,680
It was a magnificent ship in every possible way.

272
00:15:05,780 --> 00:15:06,580
She was quiet.

273
00:15:06,680 --> 00:15:08,540
She was deep running.

274
00:15:08,541 --> 00:15:11,740
Because of her speed, she could be a bear submerged.

275
00:15:11,840 --> 00:15:13,420
Because again, they're on the cutting edge.

276
00:15:13,520 --> 00:15:16,220
This is a fast ship going very deep.

277
00:15:16,320 --> 00:15:18,380
Thresher was built in Portsmouth, New Hampshire,

278
00:15:18,480 --> 00:15:20,420
and launched in 1960.

279
00:15:20,520 --> 00:15:22,720
After her first sea trials, she was brought back

280
00:15:22,820 --> 00:15:24,660
to the yard for a refit.

281
00:15:24,760 --> 00:15:28,660
Cracked welds and a number of other defects were repaired.

282
00:15:28,760 --> 00:15:30,660
With the kind of pressures that exist deep

283
00:15:30,760 --> 00:15:34,000
below the surface of the ocean, the builders and the Navy

284
00:15:34,100 --> 00:15:38,540
both knew how vitally important every weld was.

285
00:15:38,541 --> 00:15:43,540
You had to have a master welder laying down that bead of weld

286
00:15:43,640 --> 00:15:48,880
to make sure that the uniformity, right, the consistency,

287
00:15:48,980 --> 00:15:51,120
the quality of the weld is extraordinary.

288
00:15:51,220 --> 00:15:53,120
Only a sample of Thresher's welds

289
00:15:53,220 --> 00:15:56,960
were checked during construction and after her sea trials.

290
00:15:57,060 --> 00:15:59,120
Then she put out to sea again, followed

291
00:15:59,220 --> 00:16:01,900
by the submarine chaser Skylark.

292
00:16:02,000 --> 00:16:05,860
She would dive to her maximum depth in a series of steps,

293
00:16:05,861 --> 00:16:09,360
steps, then return to the surface.

294
00:16:09,460 --> 00:16:12,440
The actual maximum depth is still classified,

295
00:16:12,540 --> 00:16:16,200
but as soon as Thresher reached it, something happened.

296
00:16:16,300 --> 00:16:22,620
Just after 9 o'clock, Skylark got a transmission

297
00:16:22,720 --> 00:16:26,520
from Harvey, the captain, who said, if I remember,

298
00:16:26,620 --> 00:16:29,880
if I can paraphrase it properly, that he was experiencing what

299
00:16:29,980 --> 00:16:32,360
he called minor difficulties.

300
00:16:32,361 --> 00:16:37,360
He had positive buoyancy at an up angle,

301
00:16:37,460 --> 00:16:39,300
and he was attempting to blow.

302
00:16:39,400 --> 00:16:41,000
It was a transmission that haunted

303
00:16:41,100 --> 00:16:44,480
Naval investigators for years, a minor difficulty

304
00:16:44,580 --> 00:16:46,840
at maximum depth, and blowing the tanks

305
00:16:46,940 --> 00:16:50,020
was an emergency measure, a last resort.

306
00:16:50,120 --> 00:16:52,540
It meant forcing compressed air from flasks

307
00:16:52,640 --> 00:16:55,980
into the boat's buoyancy tanks to make her lighter than water

308
00:16:56,080 --> 00:16:58,020
so she could float upwards.

309
00:16:58,120 --> 00:17:00,260
But normally, with a nuclear power plant,

310
00:17:00,261 --> 00:17:02,760
drove the boat to the surface.

311
00:17:02,860 --> 00:17:04,740
If you do an emergency blow at test depth,

312
00:17:04,840 --> 00:17:07,200
you're going to come up like a cork, right?

313
00:17:07,300 --> 00:17:10,440
And it's very, very difficult to control the ship at all.

314
00:17:10,540 --> 00:17:12,800
And the Skylark's wondering, what in the world

315
00:17:12,900 --> 00:17:14,880
is attempting to blow?

316
00:17:14,980 --> 00:17:18,720
After the first transmission came some garbled words.

317
00:17:18,820 --> 00:17:21,040
Skylark's crew waited in growing horror

318
00:17:21,140 --> 00:17:25,380
as the silent seconds ticked by.

319
00:17:25,480 --> 00:17:27,380
Meanwhile, at the bottom of the ocean,

320
00:17:27,480 --> 00:17:30,260
the thresher's captain must have seen what was coming.

321
00:17:30,261 --> 00:17:34,600
The sub drifted deeper and deeper, helpless, without power,

322
00:17:34,700 --> 00:17:36,400
without the ability to surface.

323
00:17:36,500 --> 00:17:40,300
He could do nothing but wait as the boat sank deeper and deeper

324
00:17:40,400 --> 00:17:42,200
until finally crushed death.

325
00:17:44,840 --> 00:17:47,380
Over the underwater telephone aboard Skylark

326
00:17:47,480 --> 00:17:50,980
came the unmistakable sound of a submarine being crushed

327
00:17:51,080 --> 00:17:53,380
by the immense pressure of the ocean.

328
00:17:53,480 --> 00:17:56,360
The men aboard were killed in a fraction of a second.

329
00:17:56,460 --> 00:17:59,020
It was an engineering disaster that rocked the Navy

330
00:17:59,021 --> 00:18:01,260
that rocked the Navy to its core.

331
00:18:01,261 --> 00:18:07,060
A salvage vessel recovery, which is on the scene,

332
00:18:07,061 --> 00:18:09,160
cited an oil slick.

333
00:18:09,161 --> 00:18:13,540
So I conclude with great regret and sadness

334
00:18:13,541 --> 00:18:18,240
that this ship with 129 fine soles aboard is lost.

335
00:18:18,241 --> 00:18:21,300
What probably happened was one of those silver braised joints

336
00:18:21,301 --> 00:18:23,080
gave away.

337
00:18:23,081 --> 00:18:25,440
Now, if you're down at test depth

338
00:18:25,441 --> 00:18:28,540
and you even have a minor fracture on a silver-brazed

339
00:18:28,541 --> 00:18:31,940
joint, the water's going to come through with the power,

340
00:18:31,941 --> 00:18:35,080
you know, of a pressurized water cleaner.

341
00:18:35,081 --> 00:18:36,000
It's going to blind you.

342
00:18:36,001 --> 00:18:37,440
It's going to be very, very difficult

343
00:18:37,441 --> 00:18:40,180
to find what the source of the casualty is

344
00:18:40,181 --> 00:18:41,380
and how to contain it.

345
00:18:41,381 --> 00:18:44,400
As with most large engineering disasters,

346
00:18:44,401 --> 00:18:46,760
a whole string of unlikely events

347
00:18:46,761 --> 00:18:49,480
had to occur at the same time to destroy the Navy's

348
00:18:49,481 --> 00:18:51,540
finest submarine.

349
00:18:51,541 --> 00:18:54,080
With all her problems, even with her nuclear reactor

350
00:18:54,081 --> 00:18:56,980
Why couldn't Thresher simply blow its ballast tanks

351
00:18:57,080 --> 00:18:58,820
and float to the surface?

352
00:18:58,920 --> 00:19:02,820
The Navy tested the question on another sub, the Tenosa.

353
00:19:02,920 --> 00:19:06,120
What they found out was remarkably instructive.

354
00:19:06,220 --> 00:19:11,740
The valve system through which the compressed air had to pass

355
00:19:11,840 --> 00:19:14,960
in order to get to the ballast tanks

356
00:19:15,060 --> 00:19:17,600
was not only very narrow, we would expect that,

357
00:19:17,700 --> 00:19:21,440
it also had a cage-like device over it

358
00:19:21,441 --> 00:19:24,640
that allowed the air, as it passed,

359
00:19:24,740 --> 00:19:26,640
to leave behind any particulate matter,

360
00:19:26,740 --> 00:19:29,240
any dust or particles or whatever.

361
00:19:29,340 --> 00:19:34,180
When Tannosa tried to blow, she got a complete hard coating

362
00:19:34,280 --> 00:19:37,760
of ice on that little cage, and it stopped the blow.

363
00:19:37,860 --> 00:19:39,200
He couldn't blow.

364
00:19:39,300 --> 00:19:41,820
At maximum depth, with the nuclear power plant

365
00:19:41,920 --> 00:19:45,660
offline and leaking, that was the final straw.

366
00:19:45,760 --> 00:19:48,060
The Navy had discovered the final link

367
00:19:48,061 --> 00:19:52,560
in the long chain of events that ended in disaster.

368
00:19:52,660 --> 00:19:55,560
Without the ability to blow its tanks and surface,

369
00:19:55,660 --> 00:19:59,100
Thresher's last hope was gone.

370
00:19:59,200 --> 00:20:02,640
Two deep-sea submersibles later found and photographed

371
00:20:02,740 --> 00:20:05,520
the wreck of the Thresher 8,400 feet

372
00:20:05,620 --> 00:20:07,640
below the surface of the Atlantic.

373
00:20:07,740 --> 00:20:09,740
There is wreckage strewn along the bottom,

374
00:20:09,840 --> 00:20:12,560
including a piece with her hull number on it.

375
00:20:12,660 --> 00:20:17,460
Amazingly, the conning tower seems in perfect condition.

376
00:20:17,461 --> 00:20:21,360
Meanwhile, the Navy continued to ponder what could have happened.

377
00:20:21,460 --> 00:20:24,460
No one has actually seen a submarine implode,

378
00:20:24,560 --> 00:20:27,900
but experts believe it doesn't flatten like a crushed soda

379
00:20:28,000 --> 00:20:28,900
can.

380
00:20:29,000 --> 00:20:32,800
Rather, water at 80,000 pounds per square inch

381
00:20:32,900 --> 00:20:35,920
punches a hole into the boat, and in milliseconds,

382
00:20:36,020 --> 00:20:39,020
it blows through every partition inside.

383
00:20:39,120 --> 00:20:41,620
The boat itself retains most of its shape

384
00:20:41,720 --> 00:20:45,920
because the pressure inside and outside is equalized so fast.

385
00:20:45,921 --> 00:20:48,060
The men don't have time to drown.

386
00:20:48,160 --> 00:20:51,360
They never know what hit them.

387
00:20:51,460 --> 00:20:55,660
The story of men in the sea is longer than human memory.

388
00:20:55,760 --> 00:20:58,000
And often in history, the fate of nations

389
00:20:58,100 --> 00:21:01,140
has been decided on the decks of ships.

390
00:21:01,240 --> 00:21:04,000
But perhaps never before was the weight of the world

391
00:21:04,100 --> 00:21:08,080
carried by a small, slow, ugly, unarmed floating truck

392
00:21:08,180 --> 00:21:10,720
like the Liberty ship of the 1940s,

393
00:21:10,820 --> 00:21:14,820
ships born with a disastrous flaw.

394
00:21:14,821 --> 00:21:18,420
Liberty ships won the war for us.

395
00:21:18,520 --> 00:21:20,960
They were not meant to be pretty.

396
00:21:21,060 --> 00:21:24,260
They were meant to help save England.

397
00:21:24,360 --> 00:21:26,360
And they did.

398
00:21:26,460 --> 00:21:28,260
Lieutenant Commander Robert Montgomery

399
00:21:28,360 --> 00:21:31,500
and Captain Clark Gable are here as Irene Dunn

400
00:21:31,600 --> 00:21:34,080
smashes the traditional bottle against the ship's bow.

401
00:21:36,340 --> 00:21:41,320
441 feet long, built of steel and capable of 10 knots

402
00:21:41,420 --> 00:21:42,980
on a good day.

403
00:21:42,981 --> 00:21:45,920
The cheap, simple Liberty ships were America's answer

404
00:21:46,020 --> 00:21:49,660
to fighting two wars across two separate oceans

405
00:21:49,760 --> 00:21:51,720
at the same time.

406
00:21:51,820 --> 00:21:55,460
One of them, built by Kaiser, I believe,

407
00:21:55,560 --> 00:21:58,960
was the keel was laid day one.

408
00:21:59,060 --> 00:22:02,560
The basic hull was put on day two.

409
00:22:02,660 --> 00:22:06,840
The accoutrements, the accommodations, all the things

410
00:22:06,940 --> 00:22:09,780
that went with it, day three.

411
00:22:09,781 --> 00:22:12,280
The engine was finished day four.

412
00:22:12,380 --> 00:22:17,720
The ship was launched day five and went out with a full cargo.

413
00:22:17,820 --> 00:22:20,420
And the story is that when Hitler heard that,

414
00:22:20,520 --> 00:22:22,120
he said, we lost the war.

415
00:22:22,220 --> 00:22:24,020
Couldn't sink him fast enough.

416
00:22:24,120 --> 00:22:26,020
The tried and true riveting system

417
00:22:26,120 --> 00:22:28,540
was just too slow for this.

418
00:22:28,640 --> 00:22:32,300
But the early welded Liberty ships developed a bad habit

419
00:22:32,400 --> 00:22:34,480
that sent shivers along the waterfronts

420
00:22:34,580 --> 00:22:37,900
where sailors gossiped on piers and earned the ships

421
00:22:37,901 --> 00:22:40,640
the ship's the nickname Kaiser's Coffins,

422
00:22:40,740 --> 00:22:43,480
after the shipyard where hundreds were made.

423
00:22:43,580 --> 00:22:47,080
They had a tendency to break in half without any warning at all

424
00:22:47,180 --> 00:22:48,880
and sink within seconds.

425
00:22:48,980 --> 00:22:52,120
It was an engineering disaster.

426
00:22:52,220 --> 00:22:54,320
Welding was brand new.

427
00:22:54,420 --> 00:22:57,180
It had never been done on any great scale before.

428
00:22:57,280 --> 00:23:00,420
And here we were planning to weld the largest fleet of ships

429
00:23:00,520 --> 00:23:02,800
that ever built in the world by anybody.

430
00:23:02,900 --> 00:23:07,200
And so there were some that had a weakness right at the right

431
00:23:07,201 --> 00:23:09,660
right where the bridge began, about halfway down

432
00:23:09,760 --> 00:23:11,200
the length of the ship.

433
00:23:11,300 --> 00:23:13,700
Right at the forward end of the house

434
00:23:13,800 --> 00:23:16,840
is where the cracks would typically happen.

435
00:23:16,940 --> 00:23:19,140
It was almost explosive.

436
00:23:19,240 --> 00:23:23,880
And I believe the speed of a crack traveling

437
00:23:23,980 --> 00:23:26,420
is something like 3,000 feet per second.

438
00:23:26,520 --> 00:23:28,120
I mean, just boom, and it's over.

439
00:23:28,220 --> 00:23:30,320
You got two pieces of ship.

440
00:23:30,420 --> 00:23:33,160
Seven Liberty ships broke in half and sank,

441
00:23:33,260 --> 00:23:37,200
most in the frigid waters of the notorious Mermansk Ronde.

442
00:23:37,201 --> 00:23:39,340
above the Arctic Circle.

443
00:23:39,440 --> 00:23:41,740
40 more ships had major damage.

444
00:23:41,840 --> 00:23:45,700
Testing proved the welded construction basically sound.

445
00:23:45,800 --> 00:23:47,840
Ships had been built on a smaller scale

446
00:23:47,940 --> 00:23:49,840
using welding for years.

447
00:23:49,940 --> 00:23:53,540
There had to be another answer.

448
00:23:53,640 --> 00:23:58,620
It was found that when metallurgy tests were done,

449
00:23:58,720 --> 00:24:01,260
when the steel used in the ships, the Liberty ships,

450
00:24:01,360 --> 00:24:04,000
in particular, which wasn't the highest grade steel production

451
00:24:04,001 --> 00:24:08,000
in the war, because they weren't designed to make

452
00:24:08,100 --> 00:24:12,880
but a voyage or two, when steel was placed down

453
00:24:12,980 --> 00:24:15,820
to that temperature, it became extremely brittle.

454
00:24:15,920 --> 00:24:17,280
The frigid temperatures

455
00:24:17,380 --> 00:24:20,080
weakened the steel's internal structure,

456
00:24:20,180 --> 00:24:23,960
just as it did on the Titanic 30 years before.

457
00:24:24,060 --> 00:24:27,920
And the weakened steel intensified the strain on the welds.

458
00:24:28,020 --> 00:24:30,260
It was a deadly combination.

459
00:24:30,360 --> 00:24:33,660
The design flaw was terrifying, but the fix was simple

460
00:24:33,661 --> 00:24:35,900
and effective, if not pretty.

461
00:24:36,000 --> 00:24:39,860
A three foot wide band of steel was riveted along the ship,

462
00:24:39,960 --> 00:24:43,540
reinforcing the hull and holding her together.

463
00:24:43,640 --> 00:24:46,580
After that, there were no more catastrophic breakups,

464
00:24:46,680 --> 00:24:49,580
and the Liberty ship went on to do her job.

465
00:24:49,680 --> 00:24:52,020
If we didn't have a Liberty ship,

466
00:24:52,120 --> 00:24:55,340
the war would have lasted much longer.

467
00:24:55,440 --> 00:24:59,120
England would have been completely defeated,

468
00:24:59,220 --> 00:25:02,120
subjected, and Hitler would eventually

469
00:25:02,121 --> 00:25:04,320
have attacked America over here.

470
00:25:04,420 --> 00:25:08,120
It would come to us, attack us on our own shores.

471
00:25:08,220 --> 00:25:12,160
A staggering 400 Liberty ships were sunk by the enemy

472
00:25:12,260 --> 00:25:15,660
as they fought to prevent this.

473
00:25:15,760 --> 00:25:17,960
In the end, the repaired Liberty ships simply

474
00:25:18,060 --> 00:25:19,880
overwhelmed the enemy.

475
00:25:19,980 --> 00:25:22,080
America was just building them faster

476
00:25:22,180 --> 00:25:23,080
than they could be sunk.

477
00:25:25,880 --> 00:25:28,820
10 years after the war ended, the age of jet travel

478
00:25:28,920 --> 00:25:31,080
was still beyond the horizon, and the world

479
00:25:31,081 --> 00:25:33,180
continued to travel by sea.

480
00:25:33,280 --> 00:25:36,520
In 1956, one of the world's great luxury liners

481
00:25:36,620 --> 00:25:41,260
carrying 2,000 soles from Genoa, Italy, bound for New York,

482
00:25:41,360 --> 00:25:44,460
was ran broadside off the coast of Massachusetts

483
00:25:44,560 --> 00:25:46,360
in Pea Soup Fong.

484
00:25:46,460 --> 00:25:49,200
The other ship was a cargo vessel with an icebreaker

485
00:25:49,300 --> 00:25:51,400
bow, the Stockholm.

486
00:25:51,500 --> 00:25:54,300
The Stockholm ice-reinforced bow

487
00:25:54,400 --> 00:25:57,240
penetrated 30 feet into the Andrid Doria.

488
00:25:57,241 --> 00:26:02,240
As a result, when the Stockholm backed away,

489
00:26:02,340 --> 00:26:05,040
the gaping hole inside of the Andrea Doria

490
00:26:05,140 --> 00:26:08,120
caused the Andrea Doria to list the starboard.

491
00:26:08,220 --> 00:26:11,420
The Andrea Doria went to the bottom 10 hours later.

492
00:26:11,520 --> 00:26:14,460
Only 51 of her passengers were killed.

493
00:26:14,560 --> 00:26:16,760
Did the Great Ocean Liner have to sink,

494
00:26:16,860 --> 00:26:21,760
or was her destruction really an engineering disaster?

495
00:26:21,860 --> 00:26:24,440
The Andrea Doria was only three years old,

496
00:26:24,441 --> 00:26:28,520
at $29 million, including $1 million for original artwork,

497
00:26:28,620 --> 00:26:31,640
no expense had been spared in her construction.

498
00:26:31,740 --> 00:26:35,480
But under her shiny skin may have lurked a fatal flaw.

499
00:26:35,580 --> 00:26:39,120
The Doria carried fuel in long parallel tanks that stretched

500
00:26:39,220 --> 00:26:41,560
along either side of her hall.

501
00:26:41,660 --> 00:26:43,560
Transverse pipes between the tanks

502
00:26:43,660 --> 00:26:46,560
were designed to balance the ship as the fuel burned off

503
00:26:46,660 --> 00:26:50,640
during a voyage by flowing fuel from one tank to the other,

504
00:26:50,740 --> 00:26:52,700
keeping her level.

505
00:26:52,701 --> 00:26:55,900
After the accident, the Doria was listing 18 degrees,

506
00:26:56,000 --> 00:26:59,200
making any kind of control nearly impossible.

507
00:26:59,300 --> 00:27:02,940
In the late 50s, a controversial book called Collision Course

508
00:27:03,040 --> 00:27:04,940
claimed that if the transverse pipes

509
00:27:05,040 --> 00:27:07,740
had been usable for emergencies like this,

510
00:27:07,840 --> 00:27:10,360
fuel could have been pumped from the low-lying tanks

511
00:27:10,460 --> 00:27:12,060
into the higher ones.

512
00:27:12,160 --> 00:27:14,460
Then the ship would have floated more level in the water

513
00:27:14,560 --> 00:27:16,160
and could have been saved.

514
00:27:16,260 --> 00:27:18,200
I, too, had read Collision Course.

515
00:27:18,201 --> 00:27:24,040
I had read the fact that when the Stockholm penetrated

516
00:27:24,041 --> 00:27:26,040
into the Andrew Duyers fuel tanks,

517
00:27:26,041 --> 00:27:29,040
five on the starboard side were breached

518
00:27:29,041 --> 00:27:33,040
and five on the port side were intact.

519
00:27:33,041 --> 00:27:35,840
So the water flowed into the starboard side tanks,

520
00:27:35,841 --> 00:27:39,080
and if she was ballasted properly, port and starboard side

521
00:27:39,081 --> 00:27:42,840
tanks, that she wouldn't have sank.

522
00:27:42,841 --> 00:27:44,680
And it was plausible to me.

523
00:27:44,681 --> 00:27:47,840
Captain Mern doesn't find it plausible anymore.

524
00:27:47,841 --> 00:27:51,040
since divers have found the Doria's keel was broken,

525
00:27:51,140 --> 00:27:53,740
a usually fatal injury to a ship.

526
00:27:53,840 --> 00:27:57,720
But beyond debate is another tiny engineering flaw

527
00:27:57,820 --> 00:28:00,780
that was almost certainly at the heart of the disaster.

528
00:28:00,880 --> 00:28:03,320
Since the war, radar had become commonplace

529
00:28:03,420 --> 00:28:05,660
on all merchant vessels of any size.

530
00:28:05,760 --> 00:28:08,660
But operators weren't always at home with it.

531
00:28:08,760 --> 00:28:11,500
Radar was still fairly new at that time.

532
00:28:11,600 --> 00:28:15,240
And there was a phrase that's still common today,

533
00:28:15,241 --> 00:28:18,000
radar-induced collision.

534
00:28:18,100 --> 00:28:20,080
It meant overconfidence.

535
00:28:20,180 --> 00:28:22,940
Captain Mern and many others believe the Stockholm

536
00:28:23,040 --> 00:28:26,080
thought the Doria was 12 miles away when she was only

537
00:28:26,180 --> 00:28:29,680
two miles away because the radar's rain setting

538
00:28:29,780 --> 00:28:32,600
was not visible on the darkened bridge.

539
00:28:32,700 --> 00:28:34,500
Thinking they were avoiding collision,

540
00:28:34,600 --> 00:28:37,300
the two ships actually turned directly

541
00:28:37,400 --> 00:28:39,940
into one another's paths.

542
00:28:40,040 --> 00:28:45,240
In those days, the controls were not illuminated.

543
00:28:45,241 --> 00:28:47,780
So when you change the radar range scale,

544
00:28:47,880 --> 00:28:49,880
unless you had a flashlight to illuminate it,

545
00:28:49,980 --> 00:28:52,280
you don't know which range scale you were on.

546
00:28:52,380 --> 00:28:54,480
After the Andrea Doria disaster,

547
00:28:54,580 --> 00:28:57,680
all radars had their rain settings illuminated.

548
00:28:57,780 --> 00:29:01,280
Transverse fuel pipes might or might not have saved the Doria.

549
00:29:01,380 --> 00:29:04,900
But experts agree that a Tencent light bulb illuminating

550
00:29:05,000 --> 00:29:09,740
the Stockholm's radar range setting probably would have.

551
00:29:09,840 --> 00:29:13,000
In Hartford, Connecticut, a light snowstorm fell on the night

552
00:29:13,001 --> 00:29:17,140
on the night of January 18, 1978, a routine matter

553
00:29:17,240 --> 00:29:18,740
in a New England winter.

554
00:29:18,840 --> 00:29:21,400
But those 4 and 1⁄2 inches of wet snow

555
00:29:21,500 --> 00:29:24,280
were the straw that broke the camel's back

556
00:29:24,380 --> 00:29:26,920
in an engineering disaster so colossal

557
00:29:27,020 --> 00:29:28,820
it was hard to comprehend.

558
00:29:28,920 --> 00:29:32,420
In downtown Hartford is the new civic center and its arena,

559
00:29:32,520 --> 00:29:36,020
the replacement for a massive structure with a fatal flaw.

560
00:29:36,120 --> 00:29:37,900
I assume in other parts of the world

561
00:29:38,000 --> 00:29:40,900
they may have had bigger disasters,

562
00:29:40,901 --> 00:29:42,960
but this was a very large structure.

563
00:29:43,100 --> 00:29:47,320
I mean, size-wise, it was 300 feet by 360 feet.

564
00:29:48,000 --> 00:29:52,620
It was a huge, huge structure, and it came down.

565
00:29:53,080 --> 00:29:55,160
The arena had stood for six years

566
00:29:55,161 --> 00:29:57,680
under an advanced space truss roof design,

567
00:29:58,000 --> 00:30:00,020
second largest of its type in the world,

568
00:30:00,240 --> 00:30:02,180
and considered an engineering marvel.

569
00:30:02,800 --> 00:30:04,460
Less than five hours earlier,

570
00:30:04,820 --> 00:30:07,120
thousands of fans had cheered a hockey game.

571
00:30:07,480 --> 00:30:10,240
Yet that night, under the weight of the wet snow,

572
00:30:10,241 --> 00:30:14,240
the roof suddenly folded and crashed onto the seats below.

573
00:30:14,340 --> 00:30:16,880
That was one of the first designs

574
00:30:16,980 --> 00:30:21,180
that was made relying very heavily on the computer.

575
00:30:21,280 --> 00:30:24,780
It was simply too complicated to calculate

576
00:30:24,880 --> 00:30:28,700
using the old methods of a slide rule and hand calculations.

577
00:30:28,800 --> 00:30:30,800
This was exactly the problem.

578
00:30:30,900 --> 00:30:35,240
It wasn't braced properly to form this kind of a buckling shape,

579
00:30:35,340 --> 00:30:36,900
what we call the S shape.

580
00:30:37,000 --> 00:30:39,840
It formed this kind of shape.

581
00:30:39,841 --> 00:30:42,740
And it takes much, much less force to buckle it.

582
00:30:42,840 --> 00:30:48,180
And believe me, when the load came on the structure,

583
00:30:48,280 --> 00:30:52,820
17 pounds of snow, it just didn't buckle.

584
00:30:52,920 --> 00:30:55,360
Computers were still new then, and engineers

585
00:30:55,460 --> 00:30:58,160
were not as familiar with them as they are today.

586
00:30:58,260 --> 00:31:01,460
In this case, the engineers' complex computer-aided

587
00:31:01,560 --> 00:31:03,660
calculations were correct.

588
00:31:03,760 --> 00:31:05,900
The mistake came in transferring the computer's

589
00:31:06,000 --> 00:31:09,840
results into plans the builders could use.

590
00:31:09,841 --> 00:31:12,480
The bracing system for the 30-foot roof members

591
00:31:12,580 --> 00:31:14,380
was all wrong.

592
00:31:14,480 --> 00:31:17,840
They thought it was bracing it at its midpoint,

593
00:31:17,940 --> 00:31:20,720
at the 15-foot point.

594
00:31:20,820 --> 00:31:23,720
It was a big mistake, an obvious mistake,

595
00:31:23,820 --> 00:31:26,160
and it slipped by everybody.

596
00:31:26,260 --> 00:31:34,860
The problem showed signs or showed itself at construction,

597
00:31:34,960 --> 00:31:36,900
but no one recognized it.

598
00:31:36,901 --> 00:31:41,000
They had tremendous amount of problems in trying

599
00:31:41,100 --> 00:31:42,700
to put this thing together.

600
00:31:42,800 --> 00:31:45,100
The parts didn't seem to fit, even though they were

601
00:31:45,200 --> 00:31:46,940
fabricated precisely.

602
00:31:47,040 --> 00:31:50,440
So the contractor simply forced things into place.

603
00:31:50,540 --> 00:31:52,840
And for six years, the building stood.

604
00:31:52,940 --> 00:31:56,260
It really takes a major blunder

605
00:31:56,360 --> 00:31:58,960
to have a major disaster.

606
00:31:59,060 --> 00:32:05,000
Structures, structures are very forgiving for one reason

607
00:32:05,001 --> 00:32:12,340
or another structures don't really want to come down they are redundant they tend to stay up

608
00:32:12,341 --> 00:32:20,600
they take a major major blunder to have one come down we make a mistake as a structural engineer

609
00:32:20,601 --> 00:32:26,900
it's not like you're gonna sweat a little bit because the room gets hot it's not to take

610
00:32:26,901 --> 00:32:32,900
anything away from a mechanical engineer okay but if we make a mistake you could kill people

611
00:32:32,901 --> 00:32:35,800
This is not an isolated incident.

612
00:32:35,900 --> 00:32:38,640
Other collapses during and after construction

613
00:32:38,740 --> 00:32:42,080
here in the United States have occurred in recent decades.

614
00:32:42,180 --> 00:32:45,340
The award-winning Kemper Arena roof in Kansas City

615
00:32:45,440 --> 00:32:48,420
crashed down seven months after the Hartford disaster,

616
00:32:48,520 --> 00:32:51,960
also onto an empty arena in an engineering disaster

617
00:32:52,060 --> 00:32:54,780
where weakened bolts failed during a rainstorm.

618
00:32:54,880 --> 00:32:57,860
The human factor you will never eliminate.

619
00:32:57,960 --> 00:33:02,260
I don't really care how much sophisticated computers we buy.

620
00:33:02,261 --> 00:33:06,340
we buy or we get or we use.

621
00:33:06,440 --> 00:33:13,000
The human brain of the engineer overrides everything at the end.

622
00:33:13,100 --> 00:33:16,400
The Los Angeles Aqueduct, constructed from 1907

623
00:33:16,500 --> 00:33:19,780
to its official opening in 1913, brought water

624
00:33:19,880 --> 00:33:22,320
from the high Sierra aquifer through a string

625
00:33:22,420 --> 00:33:25,280
of artificial reservoirs connected by giant pipes

626
00:33:25,380 --> 00:33:30,120
and canals 200 miles across the desert to Los Angeles.

627
00:33:30,121 --> 00:33:34,660
It allowed that small city on the coast to grow into a megalopolis.

628
00:33:34,760 --> 00:33:37,260
The massive project was built ahead of schedule

629
00:33:37,360 --> 00:33:40,600
and under budget by architect William Mulholland.

630
00:33:40,700 --> 00:33:43,300
Without the aqueduct, there would be no Los Angeles

631
00:33:43,400 --> 00:33:45,500
as we know it today.

632
00:33:45,600 --> 00:33:47,340
But there was a human price to pay

633
00:33:47,440 --> 00:33:49,640
for all that speed and economy.

634
00:33:49,740 --> 00:33:53,180
Part of the aqueduct project was a dam and reservoir system,

635
00:33:53,280 --> 00:33:56,680
including an artificial lake created by a gravity dam

636
00:33:56,681 --> 00:34:01,760
in San Francisco Canyon, 45 miles northwest of the city.

637
00:34:01,860 --> 00:34:06,060
Built from 1924 to 1926, the dam had been full for two years,

638
00:34:06,160 --> 00:34:08,260
serving as terminal water storage

639
00:34:08,360 --> 00:34:10,560
for the city of Los Angeles.

640
00:34:10,660 --> 00:34:13,860
Though it was leaking badly, no warnings were given.

641
00:34:13,960 --> 00:34:17,900
And just before midnight on March 12, 1928,

642
00:34:18,000 --> 00:34:20,200
the St. Francis Dam burst.

643
00:34:20,300 --> 00:34:24,380
12 billion gallons of water were released into the canyon,

644
00:34:24,381 --> 00:34:29,680
A wall of water 125 feet high roared downstream,

645
00:34:29,780 --> 00:34:34,460
and 450 people below the dam drowned in their beds.

646
00:34:34,560 --> 00:34:37,860
It was the worst man-made disaster in California

647
00:34:37,960 --> 00:34:41,700
history, documented by this historical recreation

648
00:34:41,800 --> 00:34:44,440
and footage from the Department of Water and Power.

649
00:34:44,540 --> 00:34:48,540
The dam is located over to my left here.

650
00:34:48,640 --> 00:34:52,780
It spanned from the point I am at, which is the right abutment

651
00:34:52,781 --> 00:34:56,480
or west abutment, cross the canyon to the left,

652
00:34:56,580 --> 00:35:00,880
which was the left abutment or east abutment.

653
00:35:00,980 --> 00:35:04,220
Little remains of the dam in this canyon today.

654
00:35:04,320 --> 00:35:08,200
Nothing marks the spot where concrete once stood 185 feet

655
00:35:08,300 --> 00:35:12,000
high and an artificial lake flooded this entire valley.

656
00:35:12,100 --> 00:35:15,140
When the reservoir started to fill,

657
00:35:15,240 --> 00:35:19,480
the water would percolate into the sides of the hills

658
00:35:19,580 --> 00:35:20,980
on each side of the dam.

659
00:35:20,981 --> 00:35:25,420
And it became saturated on the old landslide plains.

660
00:35:25,520 --> 00:35:29,220
And so at a point in time when the friction there wasn't there,

661
00:35:29,320 --> 00:35:33,360
then it started to move down and cause that left abutment

662
00:35:33,460 --> 00:35:36,000
to give away, causing eventually the dam.

663
00:35:36,100 --> 00:35:39,160
So it was sort of a sequential operation.

664
00:35:39,260 --> 00:35:41,400
Critics say the massive loss of life

665
00:35:41,500 --> 00:35:43,940
was caused not by flawed geology,

666
00:35:44,040 --> 00:35:46,800
but by Mulholland's personality.

667
00:35:46,900 --> 00:35:50,980
The dam had been leaking badly well before it collapsed.

668
00:35:50,981 --> 00:35:52,980
In fact, locals were so concerned,

669
00:35:53,080 --> 00:35:55,920
they brought the great man up there just one day

670
00:35:56,020 --> 00:35:59,160
before the fatal disaster to see for himself.

671
00:35:59,260 --> 00:36:02,800
Mulholland was called out to the dam the day

672
00:36:02,900 --> 00:36:10,260
before it failed to inspect what was significant leaking

673
00:36:10,360 --> 00:36:13,340
before the actual release of the dam occurred.

674
00:36:13,440 --> 00:36:15,400
And he assured everybody there that it was fine.

675
00:36:15,500 --> 00:36:18,680
This was normal leakage for a dam, which, of course, it was not.

676
00:36:18,780 --> 00:36:20,380
The St. Francis Dam collapse

677
00:36:20,381 --> 00:36:23,680
forced a set of laws designed to prevent such an occurrence

678
00:36:23,780 --> 00:36:25,980
again, William Mulholland himself

679
00:36:26,080 --> 00:36:29,760
was devastated by the tragedy and spent the rest of his life

680
00:36:29,860 --> 00:36:31,560
in seclusion.

681
00:36:31,660 --> 00:36:36,000
It's certainly possible that a very small factor

682
00:36:36,100 --> 00:36:40,100
can trigger a whole cascade of other factors that

683
00:36:40,200 --> 00:36:42,800
can lead to a disaster.

684
00:36:42,900 --> 00:36:46,000
Never has this been more true than in the great Northeast

685
00:36:46,100 --> 00:36:48,600
blackout of 1965.

686
00:36:48,601 --> 00:36:50,500
While it took years for engineers

687
00:36:50,600 --> 00:36:52,400
to figure out exactly what happened,

688
00:36:52,500 --> 00:36:54,340
the fact was that for half a day,

689
00:36:54,440 --> 00:36:58,340
the entire Northeast region of North America went dark.

690
00:36:58,440 --> 00:37:01,240
Phones worked, battery radios worked,

691
00:37:01,340 --> 00:37:04,540
but practically nothing else electrical did.

692
00:37:04,640 --> 00:37:06,860
The great 65 blackout, I think,

693
00:37:06,960 --> 00:37:08,960
was the wake up call really to the nation

694
00:37:09,060 --> 00:37:11,160
of the interdependence of the power grid.

695
00:37:11,260 --> 00:37:15,500
It happened November 9, 1965 at 527 PM,

696
00:37:15,600 --> 00:37:17,560
the height of rush hour.

697
00:37:17,561 --> 00:37:20,900
LaGuardia and Kennedy airports were paralyzed.

698
00:37:21,000 --> 00:37:24,960
Thousands were trapped for the next 13 hours in elevators.

699
00:37:25,060 --> 00:37:28,900
Operating rooms and hospitals went dark, traffic lights,

700
00:37:29,000 --> 00:37:32,380
subway trains filled with almost a million commuters.

701
00:37:32,480 --> 00:37:34,680
The entire city shut off.

702
00:37:34,780 --> 00:37:38,340
Unbelievably, it all started hundreds of miles away

703
00:37:38,440 --> 00:37:40,380
in the Northwoods.

704
00:37:40,480 --> 00:37:43,880
At a power substation near Niagara Falls in Ontario,

705
00:37:43,980 --> 00:37:47,260
a small electrical relay similar to a circuit breaker

706
00:37:47,261 --> 00:37:50,360
overloaded and kicked off when it shouldn't have.

707
00:37:50,460 --> 00:37:53,100
Later, it turned out the relay was marked as having been

708
00:37:53,200 --> 00:37:56,200
upgraded, but it had actually been overlooked.

709
00:37:56,300 --> 00:38:00,600
That small event triggered a massive chain reaction.

710
00:38:00,700 --> 00:38:02,400
The whole thing started very close to here.

711
00:38:02,500 --> 00:38:05,420
There was an inappropriate action

712
00:38:05,520 --> 00:38:07,620
in one of the protective relays over there,

713
00:38:07,720 --> 00:38:10,680
one of the power swing relays, which

714
00:38:10,780 --> 00:38:13,660
tripped, causing other devices to trip.

715
00:38:13,760 --> 00:38:15,460
And it was a cascading event.

716
00:38:15,461 --> 00:38:18,400
The nation's power grid is fully interconnected.

717
00:38:18,500 --> 00:38:21,700
Each substation has automated circuitry and devices

718
00:38:21,800 --> 00:38:24,500
designed to protect itself and its equipment

719
00:38:24,600 --> 00:38:28,200
from a power fluctuation occurring elsewhere on the grid,

720
00:38:28,300 --> 00:38:32,040
a fluctuation that could destroy its giant generators.

721
00:38:32,140 --> 00:38:35,220
Any sudden change in the input or the power source

722
00:38:35,320 --> 00:38:38,220
driving the generator, or in the output, which

723
00:38:38,320 --> 00:38:40,620
is the amount of power a city draws,

724
00:38:40,720 --> 00:38:44,560
can unbalance and destroy a station's generators.

725
00:38:44,561 --> 00:38:47,600
All substations are designed to disconnect from the grid

726
00:38:47,700 --> 00:38:51,940
in case of a sudden problem that endangers their generators.

727
00:38:52,040 --> 00:38:54,900
As we take and receive a signal from them,

728
00:38:55,000 --> 00:38:57,540
when they trip their breakers, they trip our breakers,

729
00:38:57,640 --> 00:38:58,880
and it just keeps going.

730
00:38:58,980 --> 00:39:01,540
The shoebox-sized relay that tripped

731
00:39:01,640 --> 00:39:04,040
affected the Sir Adam Beck substation

732
00:39:04,140 --> 00:39:07,440
on the Niagara River, which was operating at near maximum

733
00:39:07,540 --> 00:39:09,880
capacity at the time.

734
00:39:09,980 --> 00:39:13,120
The relay shut off power in one of the station's five power

735
00:39:13,121 --> 00:39:17,920
sending its 1.5 million volts onto the other four lines.

736
00:39:18,020 --> 00:39:20,400
That was far more power than they could bear.

737
00:39:20,500 --> 00:39:24,160
So their breakers tripped to protect themselves.

738
00:39:24,260 --> 00:39:26,000
Toronto went dark.

739
00:39:26,100 --> 00:39:29,200
Suddenly there was huge extra power in the system.

740
00:39:29,300 --> 00:39:32,480
It flowed back across the river into northern New York.

741
00:39:32,580 --> 00:39:34,900
Stations there couldn't adapt fast enough.

742
00:39:35,000 --> 00:39:37,180
They began to trip off too.

743
00:39:37,280 --> 00:39:41,220
What we had at that period were transmission system control

744
00:39:41,221 --> 00:39:45,520
systems that just couldn't react fast enough to that situation.

745
00:39:45,620 --> 00:39:48,560
And we also had utilities that were stretched

746
00:39:48,660 --> 00:39:51,160
in terms of their generating capacity.

747
00:39:51,260 --> 00:39:53,260
So as a result, you had a domino effect

748
00:39:53,360 --> 00:39:57,260
across Northeast America.

749
00:39:57,360 --> 00:39:59,280
The blackout began to spread.

750
00:39:59,380 --> 00:40:01,880
Buffalo, Syracuse, Albany.

751
00:40:01,980 --> 00:40:04,780
Within minutes, the whole Northeastern region of North

752
00:40:04,880 --> 00:40:07,320
America was brought to its knees.

753
00:40:07,420 --> 00:40:10,120
Rumors spread faster than the blackout.

754
00:40:10,121 --> 00:40:11,360
It was the bomb.

755
00:40:11,460 --> 00:40:12,920
It was the Russians.

756
00:40:13,020 --> 00:40:16,920
There are some people that claim UFO did.

757
00:40:17,020 --> 00:40:17,920
I'm serious.

758
00:40:18,020 --> 00:40:20,760
Some people said they saw a mysterious red light.

759
00:40:20,860 --> 00:40:23,600
Minutes later, the only system still online

760
00:40:23,700 --> 00:40:26,740
for 30 million people was Consolidated Edison

761
00:40:26,840 --> 00:40:28,180
in New York City.

762
00:40:28,280 --> 00:40:31,740
The Con Ed manager had an agonizing choice,

763
00:40:31,840 --> 00:40:34,820
disconnect from the grid and keep New York City lit,

764
00:40:34,920 --> 00:40:38,520
or stay connected and try to power the entire Northeast.

765
00:40:38,521 --> 00:40:39,560
Northeast.

766
00:40:39,660 --> 00:40:41,420
He chose the latter.

767
00:40:41,520 --> 00:40:44,420
It was a noble effort that was doomed to failure.

768
00:40:44,520 --> 00:40:47,960
12 minutes after it began in that distant forest in Canada,

769
00:40:48,060 --> 00:40:50,860
every light in New York City winked out.

770
00:40:50,960 --> 00:40:53,000
New Yorkers took the crisis in stride,

771
00:40:53,100 --> 00:40:56,540
huddling around transistor radios by candlelight.

772
00:40:56,640 --> 00:40:59,700
Nine months later, hospitals were reporting a higher than normal

773
00:40:59,800 --> 00:41:01,140
rate of births.

774
00:41:01,240 --> 00:41:04,040
Where were you in the blackout, people would ask each other.

775
00:41:04,140 --> 00:41:07,820
To this day, those who were there still remember.

776
00:41:07,821 --> 00:41:13,260
We had a cascade of various units in the power grid,

777
00:41:13,261 --> 00:41:17,160
all of whom were doing what they were told to do precisely,

778
00:41:17,161 --> 00:41:19,760
which is protect themselves from destruction.

779
00:41:19,761 --> 00:41:22,560
But of course, the net effect was that they shed all the load

780
00:41:22,561 --> 00:41:25,580
to the Northeast to make sure that they didn't,

781
00:41:25,581 --> 00:41:29,300
weren't in fact destroyed by a power overload or a power surge

782
00:41:29,301 --> 00:41:30,780
that they were not designed to handle.

783
00:41:30,781 --> 00:41:34,980
So in some sense, we designed a system that did exactly what

784
00:41:34,981 --> 00:41:37,220
we told it to do.

785
00:41:37,221 --> 00:41:40,520
Because of Con Ed's heroic delay in isolating itself

786
00:41:40,620 --> 00:41:44,760
from the grid, its generator suffered massive damage.

787
00:41:44,860 --> 00:41:48,560
Con Ed was the last major system to come back online.

788
00:41:48,660 --> 00:41:51,540
New York City had been dark 13 hours.

789
00:41:51,640 --> 00:41:54,840
Although the computer has brought significant improvement,

790
00:41:54,940 --> 00:41:59,440
the national power grid is still vulnerable to massive outages.

791
00:41:59,540 --> 00:42:02,620
And the biggest problem is that the way the outages happen,

792
00:42:02,720 --> 00:42:05,040
and consequently the ways to prevent them,

793
00:42:05,041 --> 00:42:07,640
are still not fully understood.

794
00:42:07,740 --> 00:42:10,840
Now I'd like to represent to you that 35 years later,

795
00:42:10,940 --> 00:42:14,880
we now have the grid designed as a system

796
00:42:14,980 --> 00:42:19,360
with system-wide monitoring and control so this can happen.

797
00:42:19,460 --> 00:42:21,020
Wrong.

798
00:42:21,120 --> 00:42:24,160
I like to think of engineering as the avoidance of failure.

799
00:42:24,260 --> 00:42:28,500
All of the designs that engineers try to produce

800
00:42:28,600 --> 00:42:31,660
are basically designs that will not fail.

801
00:42:31,760 --> 00:42:34,100
Major disasters are rare.

802
00:42:34,101 --> 00:42:38,580
And the reason they're rare is so many things have to line up

803
00:42:38,680 --> 00:42:40,200
for them to happen.

804
00:42:40,300 --> 00:42:42,700
And if any one thing doesn't line up,

805
00:42:42,800 --> 00:42:44,440
you don't have the disaster.

806
00:42:44,540 --> 00:42:48,280
Mankind will continue to build monuments on the Earth,

807
00:42:48,380 --> 00:42:52,360
taller and thinner and more ephemeral than ever before.

808
00:42:52,460 --> 00:42:56,560
And he will continue to try to conquer the sea, tame the sky.

809
00:42:56,660 --> 00:42:58,960
And once in a great while, our engineers

810
00:42:59,060 --> 00:43:01,760
will try something new, and it will fail.

811
00:43:01,860 --> 00:43:03,600
Or they will do something well known,

812
00:43:03,601 --> 00:43:05,640
badly, and it will fail.

813
00:43:05,740 --> 00:43:07,200
But things do fail.

814
00:43:07,300 --> 00:43:09,700
And if the exact right set of circumstances

815
00:43:09,800 --> 00:43:12,280
aligns at that precise instant of failure,

816
00:43:12,380 --> 00:43:14,440
a disaster will result.

817
00:43:14,540 --> 00:43:15,940
It has always been that way.

